ure but this does open up for some  rapid development from black so it's  fine  since black has the bishop pair here  c3 and then a bishop to d6 preparing to  castle on the king side  bishop to d3 has some options as well to  castle on the kings side at some point  castle and then queen to ct c  two so the queen and the bishop both  attacking the pawn here on h7  black has a couple options could play f5  could play g6 uh could play h6 h5  decides first for an in-between move of  rook to e8 you're gonna do it at some  point but this is jack so  buys you a move here uh knight to e2  and then h5 is the move from madness  carlson and the pawns over here if you  kind of look at it  they're protecting a lot of these  g-square so  g6 g5 and g4 all protected by the pawns  over here so another reason that you  would  take with your e-pawn here does allow  you to just protect more of the squares  that young kristoff may be looking to  attack here  bishop to e3 now has a couple options  here could castle on the king side or  the queen side knight to d7 and then  castle on  the queen side b5 bobby fischer soon as  castles on the queen side starts to  attack here  and then white plays the move  d5 now what's interesting about this is  afterwards they're obviously  interviewing the players and they ask  you on kristoff you know  this took you about 20 minutes  and he said yeah i was really trying to  figure out you know all the calculations  once the pontics here because there's a  couple options you know you could have  the bishop just takes here on b5 so  that's one you could have  bishop to h7 and then depending on where  the king moves maybe king 2 h8  this bishop still protected but then you  have rook takes here on  uh d5 and so he was just playing all  those different scenarios of what  happens once magnus carlsen takes  here on d5 and as soon as he plays d5  remember he thought about this for 20  minutes manny's carlson immediately  played  c5 and he's like okay well that was not  what i was expecting at all  uh decided to go and take with his  bishop here on b5 spanish crossing  giving up  some material for a very very strong  attack rook here to  b8 attacking this bishop  pawn to c4 and then a6 now the the  commentators on the game you have you  know kromnic and svidler you know two of  the top players  uh to play the game they thought magnus  carlson would actually play  rook takes here on e3 and after  the pawn takes to then knight to e5  great outpost in the center of the board  and they say  uh while black is down material it's  very difficult for white to actually  find compensation and you know it's  it's a pretty good position for black  here that's not what magnus carlsen  decided to do instead plays  a6 here bishop comes back to a4  and then rook to e7 now in this  variation  uh you know you can go back in the  database and see when this has been  played at top level  before this rook 2 e5 was really  the play uh rook to e7 is a novel move  that magnus carlsen seemed that he had  prepared for this  and the idea is that after knight two g3  knight to e5 great outpost as we talked  about  then the rook can come over to b7  semi-open file start to double up and  really attack  this queen side for white  now we see e4 from white rook over here  to  b7 b3 was looking at some other options  like queen to  c3 but at the end decided just go ahead  and solidify the pawn chain  with b3 and then rook b4  and duda said he was not expecting this  uh at all for magnus carlson he was  really expecting bishop here to  f5 and said he didn't even know how to  respond correctly  because he really didn't like f3 so was  pleasantly surprised once he saw  rook to b4 bishop to  d2 attacking the rook and magnus carlson  says yeah i'll just continue to give up  material  because i have a very strong attack so  trades the rook off for the bishop here  now the double pawns on the a file it's  going to be difficult for  white to hold on to this magnus carlsen  should be able to get that material back  but you know giving up that rook  that's a lot of material that magnus  carlsen is giving up does still have  the bishop pair here brings the bishop  down to f5  we have rook to e1 protecting the knight  also just giving some  air for the king to move if he needs to  pawn two h4 pawn up here to h3  uh difficult to see exactly how magnus  carlsen should continue here  obviously has given up some material so  wants to keep the pressure on  uh some of the thoughts that the the  commentators had were maybe knight to g4  trying to add more pressure h4  doesn't seem to put too much pressure on  white seemed pretty fine just playing h3  stopping that attack here  knight comes back here to g6 rook to e3  knight down here to f4 trying to attack  as much as possible pawn up here to  g4 attacking the bishop bishop swings  back to  g6 and then king to a d1 if you look at  this knight here on f4  it's kind of a nuisance it can come to  all these awkward squares at some point  check the king so uh duda just decides  go ahead and play king 2  d1 uh stop all of those threats  f5 here knight takes the  bishop here on d6 and after the queen  takes pawn to  f5 uh bishop to  h5 uh check and then you have f3  stopping that attack you can see madness  carlson with the pieces he has left and  really trying to apply pressure knowing  that he's down  in material and if as they continue to  trade off pieces it's not going to go  good for  madness carlson magnus continues with  queen  to f6 and then bishop c3 attacking the  queen  queen g5 queen up here to e4 and duda  said yeah i should just play to rook to  e1  uh did not like the queen to e4 but  plays queen to e4  queen down here g2 and then rook e1 you  can see the queen rook and rook  all along this e file getting ready to  attack  the king here queen takes here at a2 but  really it just  isolates the queen over here probably  not the best move for madness carlson  tough to get all o